title The number with six homes
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Lap index from git log --oneline | wc -l = 3374, so the council rota reads row 3374 mod 5 = 4 -- Earth and Mercury, the concrete: the one-clock naming law, Two Rooms, and the marked value. Two Rooms governed the lap directly. A doc claiming a count is speaking from the checkable room only once something binds it; the prior lap wrote "three scripts" into a REDS row and a scan comment, which put a memory in the checkable room wearing a measurement's clothes.
think Standfast first. construction/REDS.md carries no unclosed row -- %197 and %198 both read CLOSED -- so the line was not stopped and constructive work could open.
think The last three logs all recommend the four-room index fold as next crux, and the last one also names three scripts still carrying their own 24576 as one lap's worth of tidy. I took the constant first for two reasons. It is a correctness invariant rather than a sweep, so it keeps longer; and index_fold_scan.sh is itself one of the copies, so repairing the meter before running four rooms through it is the ordering that avoids sweeping with a meter that could disagree with the law.
think grep -rn "24576" tools/ read five scripts across six sites, not three: equinox_e123_living_pin_guard_scan.sh, index_fold_scan.sh, living_docs_lint_scan.sh, living_docs_roster_scan.sh, and reds_ledger_scan.sh (two sites, an echo and a -gt). Booked as REDS %199. The transferable rule is narrower than "measure": when a repair names what it is leaving behind, count the remainder with the same tool that found the fault.
think The fix is one script rather than a shared variable. tools/fixtures/living_pin_max_bytes.sh resolves the law from $(dirname "$0")/../.. rather than the caller's cwd, because living_docs_roster_scan.sh cd's into a pen partway through and would otherwise read the pen's absent law. It exits 1 with a named reason on stderr rather than defaulting -- a meter whose bound silently defaults reports green over an unmeasured tree.
think Proven from three sides in a throwaway pen before wiring it in: law absent refuses, law stating no number refuses, and a law stating 4096 returns 4096. That third case is the one that tells a reader from a hardcoded copy.
think The wall went into declared_ceiling rather than a 51st guard. The tree's own law is that when two roofs carry one name either they agree or the name does two jobs, and declared_ceiling already owns "the number comes from the seated law rather than a copy" -- its own comment said so. Same sentence, one layer down: the pages are measured, and now so is what measures them.
think Two forms, because they carry different weight. A meter that assigns the number or compares against it as a literal is deciding with a copy -- gated at zero. A comment, an assert string, or planted pen data recites it, which is honest where the number is the subject -- six lines across six files, under a ceiling that only falls.
think The exemption is a role rather than a name. A witness asserts and a control plants; a scan decides. So *_witness.rish and *_control.sh sit outside the gated reading by file-role suffix, which is what keeps the exemption from becoming the very hand-written roster REDS %187, %190, %192, %197, and %199 are all about. declared_ceiling_witness.rish pins living_pin_max_bytes=24576 deliberately, and that assert is the check that the law has not moved.
think DECLARED_LAW_ROOT was added so the control can point the reading at real git repositories in a pen. Behaviors 23-27 prove it every roster pass rather than once by a hand: an honest meter passes, an assignment refuses and names the file, a comparison refuses, the same assignment inside a witness does not read as deciding, and a comment recites under its ceiling then refuses past it.
obs My own guard caught me twice. Its first run refused the tree at 8 recitations against a ceiling of 6, and both extra lines were written by this round -- inside the comment explaining the guard, plus a third in the helper's own header once staged. All three came out rather than the ceiling going up. A guard whose ceiling you raise to pass it is a guard with a door beside it.
obs The cold roster was contaminated by my own hand. I launched it in the background and edited the tree while it ran, so declared_ceiling was measured 29 minutes later, after its own repair, and read the roster's one red at 49 of 50. A cold roster that takes 29 minutes is cold only if the tree holds still. Booked in %199 as the method half.
obs A per-file loop over 2,046 tracked tools/ sources cost 10 seconds of guard wall-clock. Batched through one xargs grep it costs 2.4, and the deciding set is a filter over the reciting set rather than a second walk. The guard's own baseline at HEAD was already 28.6 seconds, measured, so the addition is honest rather than free.
obs Booking %199 pushed the ledger to 24,887 bytes against its own 24,576 bound and its guard said so, which is the rhythm: rows %197 and %198 folded to their shelf with link depth moved from ../ to ../../ before the file was written. The headline's three numbers and its span sentence moved with the fold; reds_ledger_monotone reads 1..199 gapless.
obs living_docs_lint.rish duty 6 stopped reciting the number and names the reading instead, and the header block I was already inside swept to plain ASCII -- em-dashes, middots, and the two comparison glyphs. Migrate-on-touch, bounded to the block the lap touched.
loom guard=declared_ceiling_scan wall_s=31.0 baseline_head_s=28.6 tracked_tools_files=2046 law_decide=0 law_recite=6
loom control=declared_ceiling_control behaviors=27 assertions=50 new_behaviors=5 new_assertions=13
